      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Neurotensin receptor type 2 (NTS2) compounds display analgesic activity in animal pain models. We have identified the first high-affinity NTS2-selective antagonist (8) that is active in vivo. This study also revealed that the NTS2 FLIPR assay designation for a compound, agonist, partial agonist, and so forth, did not correlate with its in vivo activity as observed in the thermal tail-flick acute model of pain. This suggests that calcium mobilization is not the signaling pathway involved in NTS2-mediated analgesia as assessed by the thermal tail-flick model. Finally, we found a significant bias between rat and human for compound 9 in the NTS2 binding assay."},
